  "id": "COMPOUND:12208 - PROTEIN:1693",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a potential relationship between the Influenza B virus B/Hong Kong/259/2010 hemagglutinin antigen and Protein S100-A9, through multiple indirect pathways. The hemagglutinin antigen is identified as a target for Selgantolimod, which in turn targets Protein S100-A9. Additionally, the hemagglutinin antigen is also a target for 5'-deoxy-5-fluorouridine, which also targets Protein S100-A9. Furthermore, the hemagglutinin antigen is linked to a copper chaperone for superoxide dismutase, which again targets Selgantolimod and ultimately Protein S100-A9. These findings collectively indicate that the hemagglutinin antigen may interact with multiple proteins, including S100-A9, through different mechanisms. The evidence supports a possible role for the hemagglutinin antigen in modulating S100-A9, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be elucidated. The integration of these pathways highlights the complexity of the relationship, emphasizing the need for further experimental validation to confirm the direct involvement of the hemagglutinin antigen in the regulation of S100-A9.",
